.TH RADIOSSL 1
.SH NAME
radiossl \- push ssl(3) onto the radio and exchange a message
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B radiossl
.B recv
.RI [ secret ]
.br
.B radiossl
.B send
.RI [ text
.RI [ secret ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Radiossl
opens
.BR /dev/radio0/data ,
hands that file descriptor to
.IR ssl (3),
sets a shared secret and a SHA1 digest, and writes or reads one message
through the result.
.PP
It exists to run a claim the rest of this tree rests on and which
nothing had run: that an encrypted, integrity-checked link over this
radio needs no IP stack under it, because
.IR ssl (3)
is a filter over a file descriptor \-
.B "fd m"
on its control file, and
.B ssl->connect()
taking a
.B "ref Sys->FD"
\- rather than something that lives on a network.  The whole of the
demonstration is that the descriptor happens to be a radio.
.PP
The secret defaults to a constant and can be given, so that the
interesting half can be run: a receiver with a different secret must
fail, and does, with
.BR "bad digest" .
Without that, a message crossing proves only that bytes moved.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/radiossl.b
.SH EXAMPLE
Two boards on one socket, as in
.IR radiochk (1).
On the receiver
.IP
.EX
radiossl recv
.EE
.PP
and on the sender
.IP
.EX
radiossl send
.EE
.PP
which reports
.B RADIOSSL-GOT
and the text.  Run the receiver as
.B "radiossl recv wrong-key"
for the other result.
There is no address to set, because
.B /dev/radio0
has none: it is a broadcast packet device and two stations on one
channel hear each other.  Addressing is
.IR lora (3)'s,
and this runs under it as well \- over
.BI #L/ n /data
in place of
.BR /dev/radio0/data ,
with 251 bytes of payload in place of 255.
.SH BUGS
It does not authenticate.
.IR keyring (2)'s
.B auth
wants a signer and certificates; this writes the same constant into both
ends instead, which demonstrates the transport and not the
authentication.  A constant in a program is exactly what
.B doc/pycubed-plan.ms
criticises
.B cdh.py
for, and it is here for the same reason that one is: to show the shape
without the ceremony.  What is shown is that
.IR ssl (3)
attaches to a radio and that its digest rejects a message that did not
come from the other end.  What is not shown is any of
.IR keyring (2).
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR radio (3),
.IR ssl (3),
.IR radiochk (1)
